\if@filesw\immediate\write\@auxout 
   {\string\newlabel{example: lyapunov function}{{\arabic{HilbertRuleAppCounter}}{\thepage}}}\fi
    Consider the following model of a pendulum system:
    \begin{align*}
        &x_1' = x_2\\
        &x_2' = -\sin(x_1) - x_2
    \end{align*}
    Recall that a (strict) Lyapunov function is a function $V(x_1, x_2)$ such that there exists some $\gamma > 0$ satisfying:
    \begin{align*}
        &V(0) = 0\\
        &\forall \vec{x} \left(0\!<\!\left\lVert\vec{x}\right\rVert\!<\!\gamma \rightarrow V(\vec{x})\!>\!0 \& -\dot{V}(\vec{x})\!>\!0\right)
    \end{align*}
    The existence of a (strict) Lyapunov function implies the (local) asymptotic stability of $(0, 0)$. By using a quadratic template $V(x_1, x_2) = c_1x_1x_2 + c_2x_1^2 + c_3x_2^2$, the sufficiency of $V$ being a valid Lyapunov function for the ODE system can be characterized by the following $\Pi_1$ formula in $\mathcal{L}_D$ (note that $V(0, 0) = 0$ by construction):
    \begin{align*}
        \varphi(c_1, c_2, c_3) &\equiv \forall \vec{x} \left({0}\!<\!\left\lVert\vec{x}\right\rVert^2\!<\!{1} \rightarrow V(x, y)\!>\!0 \& \frac{\partial V}{\partial x_1}x_2 + \frac{\partial V}{\partial x_2}(-\sin(x_1) - x_2)\!<\!0 \right)\\
        &\equiv \forall \vec{x}\left({0}\!<\!\left\lVert\vec{x}\right\rVert^2\!<\!{1} \rightarrow V(x, y)\!>\!0 \& (c_1x_1 + 2c_3x_2)(x_2 + \sin(x_1)) -x_2(2c_2x_1 + c_1x_2)\!>\!0 \right)
    \end{align*}
    The existence of such coefficients $c_1, c_2, c_3$ can now be encoded by existentially quantifying over these variables, resulting in a $\exists\forall$ formula with the function symbol $\sin(x)$.

\if@filesw\immediate\write\@auxout 
   {\string\newlabel{example:perturbing formulas}{{\arabic{HilbertRuleAppCounter}}{\thepage}}}\fi
    Consider the formula $\varphi(x) = 2\sin(x) - 1 > 0$, with approximation $\mathcal{F} \coloneqq \{\sin(x) \mapsto z - \frac{z^3}{3!}\}$\footnote{Recall that $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}$ is a ($\text{FOL}_{\mathbb{R}}$ definition of a) function. For clarity $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}$ is represented by its action on the dummy variable $z$.}. It is then natural to perturb $\varphi$ by constructing 
    \[\tilde{\varphi}(x) = 2\left(x - \frac{x^3}{3!}\right) - 1 > 0\]
    However, it is not clear how the perturbed formula $\tilde{\varphi}(x)$ relates to $\varphi(x)$, since $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}$ is only an approximation. To handle this, we view $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}(x)$ as an \emph{interval} $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}(x) + [-\delta, \delta]$ rather than an exact value, where $\delta \in \mathbb{Q}^+$ is a parameter representing the accuracy of the approximation $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}$. Perturbations of $\varphi$ then amounts to substituting $\sin(x)$ with $\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}$ viewed as an interval function, yielding the following formula containing intervals depending on $\delta$.
    \[\tilde{\varphi}_\delta(x) = 2\left(x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + [-\delta, \delta]\right) - 1 > 0\]
     Of course, formulas containing intervals could be interpreted semantically in different ways. This article considers the following two natural possibilities:
     \begin{itemize}
         \item For \emph{every} value $z \in [-\delta, \delta]$, the corresponding formula holds. This gives the \emph{universal perturbation} $
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
$:
         \[
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
 = \forall^{\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}(x) + [-\delta, \delta]}w~2w - 1 > 0\]
         \item There \emph{exists} some value $z \in [-\delta, \delta]$ such that the corresponding formula holds. This gives the \emph{existential perturbation} $
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
$:
         \[
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
 = \exists^{\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}(x) + [-\delta, \delta]}w~2w - 1 > 0\]
     \end{itemize}
     Note that in either case, the resulting formula has the same free-variable $x$ and belongs to $\text{FOL}_{\mathbb{R}}$. Furthermore, for any $z \in \mathbb{R}$, if $\delta \in \mathbb{Q}^+$ satisfies $\lvert\mathcal{F}_{\sin(x)}(z) - \sin(z)\rvert < \delta$, then the following chain of implications hold
     \[
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\forall}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
(z) \rightarrow \varphi(z) \rightarrow 
    \ifthenelse{\isempty{\mathcal{F}}}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}\!\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
    {\mathcal{A}^{\exists}_{\mathcal{F}}\left(\varphi, \delta\right)}
(z)\]
     Finally, we note that such substitutions are more delicate when special functions are composed. E.g. suppose the term $\sin(x + \cos(y))$ appears in some formula $\psi$ with an approximation given by (recall that the domain of $\mathcal{G}$ are function terms)
     \[\mathcal{G} \coloneqq \left\{\cos(y) \mapsto 1 - \frac{z^2}{2}, \sin(x + \cos(y)) \mapsto z - \frac{z^3}{3!}\right\}\]
     After first substituting in the approximation for $\cos(y)$ and thereby introducing a (bound) variable $w$, the resulting term $\sin(x + w)$ is \emph{not in the domain} of $\mathcal{G}$. Nonetheless, it is clear that $\sin(x + w)$ should be substituted by $\mathcal{G}_{\sin(x + \cos(y))}$. As such, we define a new approximation $\mathcal{G}_{w \mapsto \cos(y)}$ which includes $\sin(x + w)$ in its domain by ``reversing'' the substitution of $w$ back into $\cos(y)$ and evaluating the approximation given by $\mathcal{G}$.
     \[\mathcal{G}_{w \mapsto \cos(y)}(\sin(x + w)) \coloneqq \mathcal{G}(\sin(x + \cos(y))) = z - \frac{z^3}{3!}\]

